fact WorkItem(id: "WI-853", description: some(value: "`anthill query -i/--import <name>` is NON-FUNCTIONAL by construction — the prefix it synthesizes is a top-level `import`, which the grammar does not admit, so EVERY use of the flag fails the parse.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (2026-07-28, HEAD ed990f3f + WI-852). Driving the built CLI on a KB that loads clean, with a VALID pattern and a VALID import:\n  $ anthill query --path good.anthill -i wi852.good \"mk(x: 1)\"\n  error: --import: syntax error near `import wi852.good`\n  error: --import: unexpected top-level node: ERROR\n  (exit 1)\nSame for `--query-file` with any `-i`. Without `-i` both work. The flag has never worked: before WI-852 the identical run printed `parse error: parse error at 0..11: unexpected top-level node: ERROR`, which named neither the file nor the flag and read as a fault in the user's pattern — which is why it went unnoticed.\n\nROOT CAUSE, in the grammar, not the CLI. `collect_queries` (anthill-cli/src/main.rs) builds the source it parses as `import {imp}\\n` per `-i` flag, then the user's text. But `import_clause` appears only in `_namespace_content` and `_sort_content` (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:209, :237) — `_declaration`, the top level reached from `source_file` (grammar.js:62-93), has NO import alternative. So a bare top-level `import` is a syntax error BY DESIGN, and the synthesized prefix can never parse. Confirmed directly: a file whose first line is `import demo` fails `anthill check` with `1:1: syntax error near `import demo``.\n\nWORK. Decide which of the two is right and say why:\n  (a) SUPPLY THE IMPORTS STRUCTURALLY. Do not concatenate text — put the import names into the ParsedFile / scan_definitions the way a namespace body's imports arrive, so the parsed source is EXACTLY the user's text.\n  (b) WRAP THE SYNTHESIZED SOURCE IN A NAMESPACE, so the imports sit where the grammar admits them. Cheaper, but it changes the scope the query pattern is resolved in — state what that does to name resolution before choosing it.\nRefusing `-i` loudly is NOT an acceptable outcome on its own: the flag is documented in `--help` (\"Import names into query scope\") and the capability is wanted; if it must be disabled temporarily, say so explicitly and file the enabling work.\n\nTWO THINGS FALL OUT, both currently blocked on this:\n  1. WI-852 left the two LOAD printers in `collect_queries` (the `scan_definitions` error loops) UNLOCATED — they print `error: {e}` with no path. The fix is `.with_path(query_file)` on the synthesized ParsedFile, but that is WRONG while the source is a concatenation: `parsed.source` is prefix + file, so with any `-i` the reported line is shifted and would then be FALSELY attributed to the file. Under (a) the source IS the file and the stamp becomes correct unconditionally.\n  2. WI-852's span-shift machinery in `located_query_parse_error` (the `--import` / shift split) exists solely because the prefix can carry faults and displace the file's spans. Under (a) `prefix_len` is always 0 and the whole helper collapses to a plain `format_located(query_file, &file_source)` — delete it then, do not leave it as dead structure.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill query --path <kb> -i <namespace> \"<pattern>\"` resolves the pattern with the imported names in scope and exits 0; a test in anthill-cli drives the flag END TO END (a query that only resolves BECAUSE of the import, not merely a non-zero solution count); a `--query-file` parse error still reports the file's own line:col (the WI-852 test `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` must stay green, or be deleted with its reason if the shift mechanism goes); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while delivering WI-852, driving `-i` to check that the new `--import` attribution branch reported the right origin. It did — and the run it reported on could not have succeeded.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-cli/src/main.rs (collect_queries), tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js if (b), rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs (scan_definitions entry) if (a)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T08:24:08Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-853", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED by a THIRD option the ticket did not list, and it is the one that makes the ticket's (a) cheap: FIX THE GRAMMAR. `import_clause` is now an alternative of `_declaration` (grammar.js), so a file's TOP LEVEL admits an import the same way a namespace / sort body does.\n\nWHY THE GRAMMAR AND NOT THE CLI. A file's top level IS a scope — `_declaration` already admits `sort` / `fact` / `rule` / `operation` outside any namespace and the loader defines every one of them in `_global` — and `import` is how names enter a scope. Admitting the declarations while refusing the import that feeds them was an asymmetry with no rule behind it; anthill-todo's own `workitems.anthill` is a top-level-fact file that could not have imported anything. The `-i` flag is then not a special case at all: it is one ordinary top-level import, and `_global` is exactly the scope `convert_query_term` resolves the pattern in.\n\n(b) REJECTED, with the reason the ticket asked for. Wrapping the synthesized source in a namespace parses, but the pattern is converted at `_global` (`collect_queries` passes `global_raw` explicitly), so an import into `ns` would not reach it — (b) would additionally have to move the conversion scope, and then a query file's rule heads and fact functors would be DEFINED under a synthetic namespace whose name can collide with a real one. It changes name resolution to buy nothing.\n\n(a) DELIVERED on top of it: nothing is concatenated any more. Each `-i` flag is parsed as its OWN source (`import <flag>\\n`) and scanned into `_global`; the pattern / query file is parsed ALONE. Both fallouts follow:\n  1. `.with_path(query_file)` is now correct unconditionally — `parsed.source` IS the file — so a LOAD error from a query file renders `path:line:col`. MEASURED: a query file whose own top-level `import nosuch.ns` sits on line 3 reports `<path>:3:8: unresolved import 'nosuch.ns'`, identically with `-i` and without. Test `a_query_file_load_error_names_the_file` drives both.\n  2. WI-852's `QuerySource` is DELETED: prefix_len, the overlap split, the span shift and `imports_covered`'s flag-range naming. One source per flag makes the range unnecessary — a span covers exactly one flag — so `--import (one of `a`, `b`)` collapses to `--import `b``. `a_malformed_import_flag_names_which_flag` (wi852) is DELETED with that reason recorded at its site; its claim (the good flag is never blamed) is asserted more strictly by `a_malformed_flag_names_only_itself` (wi853). The other 8 WI-852 tests stay GREEN, two with rewritten rationales: `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` and `a_span_merged_across_the_prefix_is_located_in_the_file` now hold BY CONSTRUCTION (a flag and the file are different sources, so no recovery node can span both) — kept, because \"cannot happen by construction\" is a claim about the current construction.\n\nMEASURED, before touching the CLI: the grammar change ALONE made `anthill query --path kb.anthill -i wi853.demo \"mk(x: ?v)\"` return `?v = 1` where it had exited 1. The CLI rewrite is what removes the shift machinery and locates the file, not what makes the flag work.\n\nSCOPE SEMANTICS, measured and pinned because it is not the file-local import other languages have: a top-level import enters `_global`, so it is visible from every scope that chains to `_global` — including a namespace IN ANOTHER FILE of the same load (`a_top_level_import_enters_the_shared_global_scope` drives exactly that, with a control). That is the same rule top-level DEFINITIONS already follow. The language has no per-file scope to attach a narrower one to; `_global` is the top. Documented in kernel-language.md §5.1 plus a `File ::= NamespaceContent*` production in the full grammar.\n\nTWO SITES THE TICKET DID NOT NAME, both found by asking who reads a scope's imports:\n  - `codegen/rust.rs`: `generate_rust_with_config` walked `parsed.items` only, so a file's top-level imports would have emitted NO `use` lines while a namespace's did — silently generating a module whose names do not resolve. Now emitted at the same altitude as `emit_namespace`'s.\n  - `parse/convert.rs`: the import collection is now ONE helper (`collect_imports`) shared by the three things that own a scope (file, namespace, sort), rather than a third copy of the same four lines.\n\nALSO REFUSED (WI-767's rule, applied to `-i`): a listing mode (`--mode sort` / `functor` / `domain`) looks its argument up by qualified name, never through the query scope, so `-i` was INERT there and silently accepted. A flag that silently does nothing is this ticket's whole defect, so it now blocks with a message. Test: `an_import_flag_is_refused_where_it_cannot_apply`, with a control that the listing still runs without the flag.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED. A LOAD error from a `-i` flag renders `error: --import `nosuch.ns`: 1:8: unresolved import 'nosuch.ns'` — the `1:8` is a position in the one-line source the CLI synthesized for that flag. It is not false and it cannot be misread as the query file (the flag prefix names the origin), but it is noise: the rule the PARSE half follows is \"one visible argument, so the flag IS the location, with no line:col\". Dropping it needs a message-WITHOUT-position rendering for `LoadError`, i.e. the same ~20-arm restructure WI-852's finding 3 recorded, so it is not done here.\n\nDIVERGENCE: scaland's `sourceFile` is still `declaration.rep` with no imports channel on its parse result, so the Scala port will refuse a top-level import. Not ported — scaland's newest referenced ticket is WI-671, ~180 behind, so a single-ticket sync there would be unrepresentative.\n\nVERIFICATION: tree-sitter corpus 184/184 (new `Top-level import (WI-853)` case covers all three forms plus a following declaration); full workspace via rustland/scripts/test.sh 3613 passed / 0 failed. New tests: `anthill-cli/tests/wi853_query_import_test.rs` (7, each with a control that fails without the flag) and `anthill-core/tests/include/wi853_top_level_import_test.rs` (4, including the not-an-`Item` pin, since an import smuggled in as an `Item` variant would be dropped by every items walk).", at: "2026-07-29T08:24:02Z")
